{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Cogar is a small unincorporated rural community in Caddo County, Oklahoma, United States.","title":"Cogar, Oklahoma"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The post office was established March 25, 1902, and discontinued September 30, 1954.","title":"Cogar, Oklahoma"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The exterior of the abandoned W.S. Kelly gas station and general store in Cogar can be seen in the 1988 movie "Rain Man".","title":"Cogar, Oklahoma"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Rain Man is a 1988 American road comedy-drama film directed by Barry Levinson and written by Barry Morrow and Ronald Bass.","title":"Rain Man"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It tells the story of an abrasive, selfish young wheeler-dealer, Charlie Babbitt (Tom Cruise), who discovers that his estranged father has died and bequeathed all of his multimillion-dollar estate to his other son, Raymond (Dustin Hoffman), an autistic savant, of whose existence Charlie was unaware.","title":"Rain Man"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Charlie is left with only his father's car and collection of rose bushes.","title":"Rain Man"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" In addition to the two leads, Valeria Golino stars as Charlie's girlfriend, Susanna.","title":"Rain Man"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Spring Creek is an unincorporated community in Caddo County, Oklahoma, United States.","title":"Spring Creek, Oklahoma"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is 4 mi south of Cogar.","title":"Spring Creek, Oklahoma"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" It has a cemetery, church, schoolhouse, and general store still standing.","title":"Spring Creek, Oklahoma"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The cemetery and church are still used.","title":"Spring Creek, Oklahoma"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The remaining general store (there were once two at the same time) is now a house.","title":"Spring Creek, Oklahoma"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" The schoolhouse is block and was built around the turn of the 20th century.","title":"Spring Creek, Oklahoma"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" The surrounding area is very sandy and features much red rock.","title":"Spring Creek, Oklahoma"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Banat is an unincorporated community in Menominee County, Michigan, and home to Holmes Township's Township Hall.","title":"Banat, Michigan"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Banat was named after the Banat (then in the Austro-Hungarian Empire) by Romanian immigrants who came to the area in the mid 19th century.","title":"Banat, Michigan"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" At Banat's peak, it had a two-room schoolhouse, church, cemetery, bus garage, township hall, tavern, inn, two general stores, gas station and a railroad station.","title":"Banat, Michigan"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The original industry was farming, though this was largely abandoned by the mid-twentieth century due to poor soil and climate conditions.","title":"Banat, Michigan"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The permanent population dwindled during the latter half of the twentieth century.","title":"Banat, Michigan"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" The school house was closed and sold in the 1960s and is now a private cottage.","title":"Banat, Michigan"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" The church was closed and demolished in the mid-1980s.","title":"Banat, Michigan"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" The bus garage was sold off along with the school house and was eventually demolished sometime around 2000 to make way for a mini-storage facility.","title":"Banat, Michigan"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" The tavern, general store and gas station were closed and demolished following the death of the last owner, Mr. Hirsch.","title":"Banat, Michigan"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Wyoming Mercantile, also known as the Aladdin General Store is a preserved small-town general store in Aladdin, Wyoming.","title":"Wyoming Mercantile"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The store, which remains in operation, was built in 1896 by Amos Robinson as Wyoming Mercantile.","title":"Wyoming Mercantile"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Robinson died the same year, and the store went to Mahlon S. Kemmerer, who placed his properties, including the Wyoming and Missouri Valley Railroad, under the Wyoming Mercantile umbrella.","title":"Wyoming Mercantile"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Railroading continued until 1927.","title":"Wyoming Mercantile"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The store has continued, serving as a post office, bar, freight station and gas station.","title":"Wyoming Mercantile"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Saddle Store is a historic general store and gas station on the east side of Arkansas Highway 289 in the hamlet of Saddle in eastern Fulton County, Arkansas.","title":"Saddle Store"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is a two-story wood frame vernacular structure with a gable roof, set parallel to and near the road.","title":"Saddle Store"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The south-facing main facade has a centered entry flanked by sash windows, with two pairs of sash windows on the second level.","title":"Saddle Store"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The store was built in 1916, and served as the community's general store until 1988.","title":"Saddle Store"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" In addition to its retail function, its upstairs space also served as a community hall, housing elections and social functions.","title":"Saddle Store"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Barry Levinson
